-- ScholarTasks SAT and ACT practice questions, written for ScholarTasks (not taken from College Board or ACT materials).
-- Safe to run twice: questions already in the bank are skipped. All start as WAITING for teacher review at /admin/questions.
insert into practice_questions (exam, subject, topic, kind, passage, question, options, answer_index, explanation, status)
select v.exam, v.subject, v.topic, v.kind, v.passage, v.question, v.options, v.answer_index, v.explanation, 'pending' from (values
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$The botanist's notes on the orchid were remarkably ______: every leaf, root and petal was described in exact detail, down to fractions of a millimeter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sparse", "hurried", "ambiguous", "meticulous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Exact detail, down to fractions of a millimeter" describes very careful, precise work, which is what meticulous means.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$Although the town council expected the new bike lanes to be controversial, residents' response was largely ______: at the public meeting, most speakers praised the plan and only a few raised concerns.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["favorable", "hostile", "confused", "indifferent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Most speakers praised the plan, so the response was positive (favorable). "Although" signals the contrast with the expected controversy.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$Early critics dismissed the novelist's first book as derivative, but later scholars found it strikingly ______, noting that it used storytelling techniques no earlier writer had tried.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["conventional", "repetitive", "original", "obscure"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"But" signals the opposite of derivative (copied), and "techniques no earlier writer had tried" points to original.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$The engineer's proposal was ______: it used only materials the company already had in stock and could be finished within the existing budget.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["extravagant", "theoretical", "reckless", "practical"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Using materials on hand and staying on budget describe a sensible, workable plan: practical.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$The documentary's narrator speaks in a calm, ______ tone, letting the dramatic footage of the storm speak for itself rather than adding excitement with her voice.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sarcastic", "boastful", "restrained", "frantic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$She avoids adding excitement, so her tone holds back: restrained. "Calm" supports this.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$Many people assume that octopuses, being solitary, have little need for complex communication. Yet researchers filming a site off the coast of Australia recorded octopuses changing skin color and posture in response to one another, sometimes in ways that seemed to prevent fights. The findings suggest that octopus social life may be richer than once believed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It presents a common assumption, describes evidence that challenges it, and states a conclusion drawn from that evidence.", "It compares two species of octopus and explains why one is more social.", "It summarizes a debate between two researchers and declares one of them correct.", "It describes an experiment, explains its flaws, and proposes a better method."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sentence 1 is the assumption, "Yet" introduces the challenging evidence, and the last sentence draws the conclusion.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$Mae Jemison, the first African American woman to travel to space, trained as a physician and served as a medical officer in Sierra Leone and Liberia before joining NASA. After leaving NASA, she founded a technology company and has led efforts to encourage students, especially girls, to pursue science.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare Jemison's career with the careers of other astronauts", "To argue that doctors make the best astronauts", "To describe the range of one person's accomplishments in medicine, space exploration and education", "To explain how NASA selects its astronauts"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text lists her work as a doctor, an astronaut and a leader in science education. It doesn't explain selection, argue, or compare.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$The city's oldest library was nearly torn down in the 1970s. Local volunteers raised money for repairs and held readings on its steps to draw attention to the building. Today it is one of the busiest branches in the system.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?$q$, $q$["It questions whether the library should have been saved.", "It explains how the library avoided the fate mentioned in the first sentence.", "It gives the reason the library was nearly torn down.", "It describes how the library is used today."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The first sentence says the library was almost torn down; the second tells what people did to save it.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Cross-text connections$q$, 'mc', $q$Text 1: Some nutrition researchers argue that eating breakfast is essential for students' concentration, pointing to studies in which students who ate breakfast scored higher on morning tests.
Text 2: Other researchers note that students who eat breakfast also tend to sleep more and have more regular routines. Without accounting for these differences, they argue, it is impossible to say whether breakfast itself improves concentration.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the studies described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would agree that breakfast is the main cause of better concentration.", "They would say the studies do not rule out other explanations for the higher scores.", "They would argue that morning tests are an unfair way to measure learning.", "They would say the studies prove that sleep matters more than breakfast."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2's point is that sleep and routines could explain the scores, so the studies don't show breakfast is the cause. They don't claim to prove sleep matters more.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Central ideas$q$, 'mc', $q$When the river flooded in the spring of 1927, farmers in the valley lost crops, homes and livestock. In the years that followed, however, many of them formed cooperatives, pooling equipment and sharing labor so that no family had to rebuild alone. Historians credit these cooperatives with helping the valley recover faster than neighboring regions.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Farmers who worked together after a flood helped their region recover relatively quickly.", "The flood of 1927 was the worst disaster in the region's history.", "Historians disagree about what caused the 1927 flood.", "Farm cooperatives were common in the valley before 1927."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text moves from the flood's damage to the cooperatives and credits them with a faster recovery. The other choices aren't stated.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$"Harbor Morning" is a poem written for this practice set. A student claims that the speaker of the poem sees the sea as both comforting and threatening.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the poem most effectively illustrates the student's claim?$q$, $q$["\"Each morning the fishing boats leave the harbor.\"", "\"The water is colder in March than in May.\"", "\"The gulls cry out above the gray stones.\"", "\"The tide rocks me like a cradle, then pulls at my feet like a thief.\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Like a cradle" is comforting and "like a thief" is threatening, so this line shows both sides of the claim.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$A student surveyed 200 students at her school about how they usually get to school. Walk: 30. Bus: 90. Car: 60. Bike: 20. The student concludes that the bus is the most common way to get to school, used by nearly half of those surveyed.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the survey to support the student's conclusion?$q$, $q$["Walking and biking together account for 50 students.", "90 of the 200 students said they ride the bus, more than for any other choice.", "60 students said they ride in a car, which is more than the number who walk.", "Only 20 students said they bike to school."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is about the bus being most common and near half: 90 of 200 is 45%, the largest group.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$Average daily water use per household in a town (gallons): 2015: 310. 2018: 285. 2021: 260. 2024: 240.$q$, $q$Which choice most accurately describes the data?$q$, $q$["Average daily water use rose between 2018 and 2021.", "Average daily water use was the same in 2015 and 2024.", "Average daily water use dropped by more than half between 2015 and 2024.", "Average daily water use fell in each period shown, from 310 gallons in 2015 to 240 gallons in 2024."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Each number is lower than the one before. A drop from 310 to 240 is 70 gallons, far less than half.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$Bees that visit a patch of flowers seem to remember which flowers recently offered nectar. In one experiment, researchers refilled some artificial flowers with sugar water every few minutes and left others empty. Over time, bees visited the refilled flowers far more often than the empty ones. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["bees visit flowers in a random order.", "bees can learn and use information about which food sources are likely to be rewarding.", "bees prefer artificial flowers to real ones.", "sugar water is more nutritious for bees than natural nectar."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Bees shifted toward the flowers that kept rewarding them, which shows learning. Real flowers and nutrition weren't compared.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$In a study of city parks, researchers found that parks with more tree cover had lower afternoon temperatures than parks with little tree cover, even when the parks were the same size and in the same neighborhood. Because size and location were held constant, the researchers concluded that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["larger parks are always cooler than smaller parks.", "neighborhoods with parks are cooler than neighborhoods without parks.", "afternoon temperatures do not vary from park to park.", "tree cover, rather than park size or neighborhood, likely explains the temperature difference."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Holding size and location the same rules them out, leaving tree cover as the likely cause.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$The marine biologist spent six months studying coral reefs off the coast of ______ she found that warmer water caused several species to lose their color.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Belize and,", "Belize,", "Belize", "Belize, and"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two complete sentences joined with "and" need a comma before "and." A comma alone is a comma splice; nothing at all is a run-on.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$The recipe calls for only three ______ flour, water and salt.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["ingredients;", "ingredients:", "ingredients that", "ingredients, and"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon introduces a list after a complete sentence. A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and the list isn't one.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$The storm knocked out power across the ______ crews worked through the night to restore it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["county,", "county, and,", "county", "county;"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both sides are complete sentences, so a semicolon can join them. A comma alone would be a comma splice.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$The collection of handwritten letters, which spans more than forty years of the family's history, ______ now on display at the county museum.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been", "is", "were", "are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "collection" (singular), not "letters," so the verb is "is." Skip the phrases between subject and verb.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$When the two companies merged, the new board asked each department to review ______ budget and suggest savings.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their", "its", "they're", "it's"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Each department" is singular and owns the budget, so the possessive "its" fits. "It's" means "it is."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$By the time the rescue team reached the summit last night, the climbers ______ there for more than six hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are waiting", "had been waiting", "will have waited", "have been waiting"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The waiting happened before another past event (the team reaching them), so use the past perfect: "had been waiting."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$The three ______ science projects were displayed in the main hallway, where visitors could vote for a favorite.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["students", "student's", "students's", "students'"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Three students own the projects: plural possessive, "students'."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$Walking through the museum's newest exhibit, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["visitors can see fossils that are more than 100 million years old.", "more than 100 million years is the age of the fossils.", "the fossils are more than 100 million years old.", "fossils more than 100 million years old can be seen by visitors."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The opening phrase describes who is walking, so "visitors" must come right after the comma. Otherwise the fossils seem to be walking.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$Solar panels can lower a household's electricity bills. ______ installing them can cost thousands of dollars up front, which is a barrier for many families.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,", "Therefore,", "However,", "For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence gives a drawback that contrasts with the benefit, so "However" fits.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$Hummingbirds have extremely fast metabolisms. ______ they must eat often throughout the day, sometimes visiting hundreds of flowers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,", "For instance,", "As a result,", "In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Eating often is a consequence of the fast metabolism: cause and effect, "As a result."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$The first draft of the report was nearly fifty pages long. ______ the final version, after careful editing, was only twelve pages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,", "In addition,", "Thus,", "By contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Fifty pages versus twelve is a contrast.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• The Okefenokee Swamp is in southeastern Georgia and northern Florida.
• It covers about 438,000 acres.
• It is one of the largest blackwater swamps in North America.
• Much of it is protected as a national wildlife refuge.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the size of the Okefenokee Swamp. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Covering about 438,000 acres, the Okefenokee Swamp is one of the largest blackwater swamps in North America.", "Much of the Okefenokee Swamp is protected as a national wildlife refuge.", "The Okefenokee Swamp is in southeastern Georgia and northern Florida.", "The Okefenokee Swamp, in Georgia and Florida, is a protected area."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only this choice uses the size details (acres and "one of the largest"). Match the goal, not just the facts.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• Katherine Johnson was a mathematician at NASA.
• She calculated flight paths for early space missions.
• Before his 1962 orbital flight, astronaut John Glenn asked that she personally check the computer's calculations.
• She received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2015.$q$, $q$The student wants to show how much astronauts trusted Johnson's work. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Before his 1962 orbital flight, John Glenn asked that Katherine Johnson personally check the computer's calculations.", "Katherine Johnson, a NASA mathematician, received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2015.", "Katherine Johnson was a mathematician who worked at NASA.", "Katherine Johnson calculated flight paths for early space missions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An astronaut asking for her to double-check the computer shows trust. The other choices are true but don't show trust.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$The new bridge design was praised for its ______: it used half as much steel as the old design yet could carry twice the load.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["efficiency", "extravagance", "obscurity", "fragility"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Doing more (twice the load) with less (half the steel) is efficiency.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$Because the historian had only a few damaged letters to work with, her conclusions about the merchant's life were necessarily ______, and she noted that new evidence might change them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["definitive", "irrelevant", "tentative", "exaggerated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Limited evidence and a note that new evidence could change her view mean her conclusions are tentative (not final).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$The volunteer coordinator was known for being ______: she answered every email within an hour and never missed a scheduled shift.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indecisive", "dependable", "aloof", "careless"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Fast replies and never missing shifts describe someone you can count on: dependable.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$Some desert plants survive long droughts by entering a ______ state, slowing their growth almost to a stop until rain returns.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["flourishing", "decorative", "frantic", "dormant"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Growth slowing almost to a stop is an inactive, resting state: dormant.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$Critics described the singer's new album as ______, noting that it mixed folk, jazz and electronic music in ways that rarely seemed forced.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["eclectic", "monotonous", "inaudible", "imitative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Drawing on many different styles is eclectic. Monotonous would mean all the same.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$The museum's new exhibit was intended to ______ public interest in local history, and attendance at the historical society's lectures doubled the following year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disguise", "suppress", "stimulate", "postpone"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Attendance doubling shows the exhibit increased interest: stimulate.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$Although the committee's report was long, its central recommendation was ______: the city should replace its oldest water pipes within five years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unintelligible", "contradictory", "convoluted", "straightforward"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Although the report was long" sets up a contrast: the main point was simple and clear.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$The chef insisted that cooking was less about following recipes than about ______: tasting, adjusting and adapting to whatever ingredients were fresh that day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["improvisation", "memorization", "measurement", "repetition"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tasting, adjusting and adapting on the spot is improvisation.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$Researcher Ana Ortiz's study of river otters was notably ______: she observed the same family groups every week for nine years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["secretive", "sustained", "careless", "brief"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Nine years of weekly observation is long-lasting: sustained.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$In her memoir, the architect recalls that her early designs were often ______ by senior partners, who rejected them as too unusual to build.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["copied", "constructed", "dismissed", "celebrated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rejected as too unusual means her designs were dismissed.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$The town's annual fair began in 1952 as a modest gathering of a few farm families. Over the decades it grew steadily, and today the fair draws visitors from across the region.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word "modest" most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["shy", "inexpensive", "humble in manner", "small"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"A few farm families" shows modest here means small in size, not a personality trait.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Words in context$q$, 'mc', $q$To prepare for the debate, Marcus had to marshal his evidence carefully, arranging his facts and examples so that each one supported the next.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word "marshal" most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["escort", "command", "question", "organize"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Arranging facts so each supports the next means organizing them.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$For years, scientists believed that the giant panda's diet of bamboo provided almost no protein. Recent studies of panda droppings, however, found that the bamboo shoots pandas eat in spring are surprisingly rich in protein, giving the animals a diet closer to that of meat eaters than once thought.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes a problem and then proposes a solution to it.", "It describes an earlier belief and then presents findings that revise it.", "It compares pandas with other bears and concludes they are unrelated.", "It explains a method and then lists its steps in order."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"For years, scientists believed" gives the old view; "however" introduces findings that change it.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$Many people picture glaciers as frozen and still. In fact, glaciers flow downhill under their own weight, some moving more than a meter a day. As they move, they grind the rock beneath them, carving valleys over thousands of years.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To correct a common misunderstanding about glaciers and describe how they shape land", "To compare glaciers on different continents", "To argue that glaciers are disappearing faster than expected", "To explain how scientists measure glacier speed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text starts with a mistaken picture ("frozen and still") and then explains how glaciers really move and carve valleys.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$The following text is adapted from a 1910 short story written for this practice set.
The old clock in the hall had not kept proper time for years. Still, every evening Grandfather wound it with great care, as though it were a promise he did not intend to break.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe the layout of the house", "To criticize Grandfather for wasting time", "To show that Grandfather's care for the clock matters to him more than its usefulness", "To explain how old clocks work"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clock doesn't work, yet he winds it "like a promise," so the habit means something to him beyond keeping time.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$Urban gardens are often praised for supplying fresh vegetables. Researcher Kofi Mensah points out a less obvious benefit: in his surveys of three cities, residents who worked in community gardens reported knowing more of their neighbors than residents who did not.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It challenges the claim that urban gardens supply fresh vegetables.", "It gives a reason urban gardens are disappearing.", "It introduces an additional benefit of urban gardens beyond the one mentioned first.", "It explains why some residents refuse to garden."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first sentence names a known benefit; the second adds "a less obvious benefit" (knowing neighbors).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$Some economists argue that a four-day workweek lowers productivity. A trial at several companies offers a different picture: workers on a four-day schedule completed about the same amount of work as before and reported less stress. Still, the trial lasted only six months, so its long-term effects remain unknown.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the last sentence in the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It points out a limitation of the evidence presented.", "It explains why workers felt less stress.", "It restates the economists' argument.", "It gives the main finding of the trial."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Still, the trial lasted only six months" admits a weakness in the evidence.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Text structure and purpose$q$, 'mc', $q$Before the invention of refrigerators, many American households kept food cold with blocks of ice cut from frozen lakes in winter. The ice was stored in thick-walled ice houses, packed in sawdust, and could last well into summer. An entire industry grew up to harvest, store and deliver it.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that ice houses were better than refrigerators", "To show that sawdust was expensive", "To describe how people kept food cold before refrigerators", "To explain why lakes freeze in winter"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Every sentence describes the ice-harvesting system used before refrigerators.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Cross-text connections$q$, 'mc', $q$Text 1: Some city planners argue that adding lanes to crowded highways is the best way to reduce traffic jams. More lanes, they say, give cars more room to move.
Text 2: Transportation researchers have repeatedly found that new lanes fill up within a few years, because easier driving encourages more people to drive. They call this effect "induced demand."$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the planners in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would say added lanes may relieve traffic only briefly, because more people will start driving.", "They would argue that traffic jams are caused mainly by accidents.", "They would agree that adding lanes is the best long-term solution.", "They would say that highways should have fewer lanes than they do now."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2 says new lanes "fill up within a few years," so any relief from added lanes is short-lived.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Cross-text connections$q$, 'mc', $q$Text 1: Historian Lena Park argues that the printing press spread new ideas mainly by making books cheaper, so more people could own them.
Text 2: Historian David Cole agrees that books became cheaper but argues that the press mattered even more because it made texts identical. When every copy said the same thing, scholars in different cities could debate the same exact words.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Cole most likely respond to Park's claim?$q$, $q$["He would reject her claim that books became cheaper.", "He would say that scholars rarely read printed books.", "He would accept her point about cost but argue that identical copies were even more important.", "He would argue that the printing press did not spread new ideas."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Cole "agrees that books became cheaper" but says identical texts "mattered even more."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Central ideas$q$, 'mc', $q$Honeybees perform a "waggle dance" to tell other bees where to find food. The angle of the dance shows the direction of the food compared with the sun, and the length of the waggle shows the distance. Using this information, other bees can fly straight to a flower patch they have never visited.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Honeybees dance to attract mates in the spring.", "Honeybees use a dance to share the direction and distance of food sources.", "The sun is the most important source of energy for honeybees.", "Honeybees can only find food near their hives."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Each sentence explains how the waggle dance tells other bees where food is.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Central ideas$q$, 'mc', $q$The following text is adapted from a story written for this practice set.
Maya had practiced the piano piece for weeks, but at the recital her hands shook and she missed the first chord. She took a breath, started again, and played the rest without a mistake. Afterward, her teacher said the restart was the best part.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Maya recovers from a nervous start and finishes her performance well.", "Maya chooses a new piece for the recital.", "Maya decides to quit playing the piano.", "Maya's teacher is disappointed in the performance."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$She stumbles, restarts and plays the rest perfectly, and her teacher praises the recovery.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Central ideas$q$, 'mc', $q$Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of all known marine species. Reefs provide food and shelter for fish, and they also protect coastlines by breaking up waves before they reach the shore.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Fish are the only animals that live on coral reefs.", "Waves are the main threat to coral reefs.", "Coral reefs are small in area but very important to ocean life and coastlines.", "Coral reefs cover most of the ocean floor."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text contrasts reefs' small size with their large value for species and shorelines.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Central ideas$q$, 'mc', $q$For decades, scientists thought that adult human brains could not grow new neurons. Studies beginning in the 1990s found evidence that new neurons do form in at least one brain region, the hippocampus, which is involved in memory. Researchers still debate how many new neurons adults produce and what role they play.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Adult brains grow new neurons in every region at the same rate.", "Evidence suggests adult brains can grow some new neurons, though scientists still debate the details.", "The hippocampus has no role in memory.", "Scientists have stopped studying the adult brain."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text moves from an old belief to new evidence and ends with ongoing debate.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Central ideas$q$, 'mc', $q$The following text is adapted from a story written for this practice set.
Every Saturday, Mr. Reyes opened his hardware store an hour early so that the farmers could buy supplies before the heat set in. He never advertised the early hours, and he never charged extra. "They work hard," he said. "The least I can do is unlock a door."$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The farmers complain that the store opens too late.", "Mr. Reyes is trying to earn more money by opening early.", "Mr. Reyes quietly goes out of his way to help the farmers in his community.", "Mr. Reyes plans to close his store."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$He opens early without advertising or charging extra, just to help: quiet kindness.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Central ideas$q$, 'mc', $q$In the 1820s, the Cherokee silversmith Sequoyah created a writing system for the Cherokee language. Within a few years, a large share of Cherokee people could read and write in their own language, and the nation began publishing a newspaper, the Cherokee Phoenix, in both Cherokee and English.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Sequoyah's writing system quickly made reading and writing widespread among the Cherokee.", "The Cherokee language had a writing system for many centuries before the 1800s.", "The Cherokee Phoenix was the first newspaper in the United States.", "Sequoyah wrote most of the articles in the Cherokee Phoenix."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text tells how Sequoyah's system led to widespread literacy and a newspaper. The other choices are not stated.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$A biologist hypothesizes that a species of frog calls more often on humid nights than on dry nights because humid air helps sound travel farther.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the biologist's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Frogs recorded on humid nights called about twice as often as frogs recorded on dry nights.", "Larger frogs had louder calls than smaller frogs.", "The frogs called more often in the spring than in the fall.", "The frogs were more active near ponds than near streams."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The hypothesis is about humid versus dry nights. Only this choice compares calling on those two kinds of nights.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$A student claims that in the novel "Lanterns" (written for this practice set), the narrator, June, grows more confident over the course of the story.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the novel would best support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["June's brother \"always ate breakfast standing up.\"", "Early on, June \"spoke so softly that no one turned around\"; by the last chapter, she \"stood and addressed the whole town.\"", "June \"lived in a narrow house at the end of Willow Street.\"", "The town \"lit lanterns every winter to mark the longest night.\""]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only this choice shows a change in June from shy to bold.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$Percent of students at a high school who read for fun at least once a week: 9th grade: 48%. 10th grade: 41%. 11th grade: 35%. 12th grade: 30%. A student concludes that reading for fun becomes less common in each higher grade at the school.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the survey to support the student's conclusion?$q$, $q$["The percent who read for fun falls in each grade, from 48% in 9th grade to 30% in 12th grade.", "30% of 12th graders read for fun at least once a week.", "The percent for 11th grade is between the percents for 10th and 12th grades.", "More than 40% of 10th graders read for fun."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is about a decline across every grade, so the answer must show all four values falling.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$Average monthly rainfall in a town (inches): March: 4.1. April: 3.6. May: 3.2. June: 4.8. A student claims that June was the wettest of these months.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data that support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["March had more than 4 inches of rain.", "June's average rainfall, 4.8 inches, is higher than that of March, April or May.", "April's average rainfall was 3.6 inches.", "Rainfall decreased from March to May."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$To show June was wettest, compare June with all the other months.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$Researchers tested whether background music affects how well people remember a list of words. Group A heard no music: 14 words remembered on average. Group B heard music without lyrics: 13 words. Group C heard music with lyrics: 9 words. The researchers concluded that music with lyrics may interfere with memorizing words.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the study to support the researchers' conclusion?$q$, $q$["All three groups remembered at least 9 words.", "Group A remembered 14 words on average.", "Group C, which heard music with lyrics, remembered about 9 words, fewer than either other group.", "Groups A and B remembered a similar number of words."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The conclusion is about lyrics, so the best evidence shows the lyrics group did worst.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$A student claims that in the poem "Night Shift" (written for this practice set), the speaker feels lonely while working at night.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the poem best supports the student's claim?$q$, $q$["\"The bus arrives at nine and leaves at nine-oh-five.\"", "\"I count the empty chairs, and no one counts with me.\"", "\"Outside, the street lamps flicker on.\"", "\"The coffee machine hums its single note.\""]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Empty chairs and "no one counts with me" show the speaker is alone and feels it.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Command of evidence$q$, 'mc', $q$Number of bird species counted at a wetland after it was restored: Year 1: 22. Year 2: 31. Year 3: 38. Year 4: 41. An ecologist concludes that the restoration attracted more bird species each year, though the increase slowed over time.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data to support the ecologist's conclusion?$q$, $q$["The count rose every year, by 9, then 7, then 3 species.", "41 species were counted in Year 4.", "The wetland had 22 species in its first year.", "The count in Year 2 was higher than in Year 1."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both parts of the claim (rising every year, slower increases) are shown by the yearly gains 9, 7, 3.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$Ancient Roman concrete has survived for about 2,000 years, while much modern concrete cracks within decades. Researchers recently found small lumps of lime in Roman concrete. When cracks form and water seeps in, the lime dissolves and then hardens again, filling the cracks. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["Roman builders used more water than modern builders.", "Roman buildings were never exposed to rain.", "Roman concrete may last longer partly because it can repair its own cracks.", "modern concrete contains no lime at all."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Lime that fills cracks when water gets in is a self-repair process, which would help explain the long life.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$In a study, students who took handwritten notes remembered more ideas from a lecture a week later than students who typed their notes. The researchers observed that typists tended to copy the lecture word for word, while handwriters, who could not write as fast, had to summarize in their own words. The researchers therefore suggested that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["typing is always harmful to learning.", "handwriting is faster than typing.", "putting ideas in one's own words may help people remember them.", "students should not take notes during lectures."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The difference the researchers point to is summarizing versus copying, so summarizing likely helps memory.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$Archaeologists found seeds of a plant that grows only in warm, wet climates in a cave in a region that is now dry and cool. Radiocarbon dating showed the seeds were about 8,000 years old. Because the seeds appear to have come from plants that grew nearby, the archaeologists concluded that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people in the region grew no other plants.", "the region's climate was likely warmer and wetter about 8,000 years ago.", "the plant has since adapted to dry, cool climates.", "radiocarbon dating does not work on seeds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If the plant grew nearby and needs warm, wet conditions, the region must have been warm and wet then.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$Some species of birds that live on islands without predators have lost the ability to fly. Flying takes a great deal of energy, and on islands with no animals to escape from, birds that walk can survive just as well. This pattern suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["flight may be lost over generations when it is no longer needed for survival.", "birds on islands are larger than birds on the mainland.", "predators were brought to every island by people.", "all island birds eventually stop flying."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text links losing flight to the absence of predators, so flight fades when it isn't needed.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$A bakery noticed that sales of its whole-wheat bread rose sharply in the same month it moved the bread from a bottom shelf to a shelf at eye level. Nothing else about the bread, including its price, changed. The owner reasonably concluded that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["sales would have risen no matter where the bread was placed.", "placing the bread where customers could see it easily likely increased sales.", "customers prefer whole-wheat bread to all other breads.", "the bread's price was too low before."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The only change was shelf position, so that is the likely cause.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$Octopuses have about 500 million neurons, but roughly two-thirds of them are located in the arms rather than in the brain. In experiments, an octopus arm that has been separated from the body can still respond to touch and grab pieces of food. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["octopuses have fewer neurons than most fish.", "octopus arms work only when the brain sends signals first.", "an octopus's arms can respond to their surroundings with little direction from its brain.", "octopuses have no brain."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An arm that responds without any connection to the brain must be able to act partly on its own.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$The library's new hours are designed for working ______ it now stays open until 10 p.m. on weekdays.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["families:", "families", "families,", "families, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon can join two complete sentences when the second explains the first. A comma alone is a comma splice.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$Rosalind Franklin's X-ray images of DNA ______ especially the one known as Photo 51, helped other scientists determine the molecule's double-helix shape.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["molecules", "molecules;", "molecules,", "molecules:"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Especially the one known as Photo 51" is an extra phrase, so it needs commas on both sides.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$The team tested three materials for the ______ cotton, wool and a recycled plastic fabric.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["jacket lining;", "jacket lining. These were", "jacket lining:", "jacket lining,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A colon introduces a list after a complete sentence. A semicolon can't introduce a list.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$Many people think the tomato is a ______ botanists classify it as a fruit because it develops from a flower and contains seeds.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["vegetable, however,", "vegetable however", "vegetable; however", "vegetable; however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"However" joins two complete sentences here, so it needs a semicolon before it and a comma after it.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$The marathon runner, who had trained for two ______ finished the race in under three hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["years,", "years:", "years", "years;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Who had trained for two years" is an extra clause set off by commas, so a comma must close it.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$When the power went out during the storm, the ______ kept the restaurant open by cooking on a gas stove and serving meals by candlelight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["owners,", "owners:", "owners;", "owners"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$No punctuation belongs between a subject ("owners") and its verb ("kept").$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$Astronomers have discovered thousands of planets orbiting other ______ many of them are larger than Jupiter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["stars,", "stars, and", "stars and,", "stars"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two complete sentences joined with "and" need a comma before "and."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 'mc', $q$My uncle's favorite saying is ______ "Measure twice, cut once."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["simple:", "simple;", "simple,", "simple. That"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon introduces the quotation that explains what the saying is, after a complete sentence.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$The results of the survey, which was sent to more than 3,000 households, ______ that most residents support the new park.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has shown", "show", "shows", "is showing"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "results" (plural), so the verb is "show."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$Neither the coach nor her assistants ______ expected the team to win the championship.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has", "is", "had", "was"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$With "neither... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "assistants" (plural). "Had" works for plural; the others are singular.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$Each of the paintings in the gallery ______ a small card describing the artist and the year it was made.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has", "have", "were having", "are having"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Each" is singular, so the verb is "has."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$When the scientists published their findings, ______ were surprised by how quickly other labs confirmed them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["one", "they", "it", "he"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The pronoun refers to "the scientists" (plural): "they."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$Last summer, my sister ______ a job at a veterinary clinic, where she learned to care for injured animals.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been taking", "will take", "takes", "took"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Last summer" sets the action in the past, so use the simple past: "took."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$Having studied the old maps for weeks, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the researcher finally located the lost village.", "the lost village was finally located by the researcher.", "finally, the lost village was found.", "the old maps finally showed the lost village."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The person who studied the maps must come right after the comma: "the researcher."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$The company's founder, along with two engineers, ______ the first prototype in a garage.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were building", "have built", "are building", "built"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Along with two engineers" doesn't change the subject, "founder." "Built" is correct for a singular past subject; the others are plural or the wrong tense.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$The ______ decision to extend the deadline gave students an extra week to finish their projects.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["teacher's'", "teachers'", "teacher", "teachers"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Several teachers made the decision, so use the plural possessive "teachers'."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Form, structure and sense$q$, 'mc', $q$If I ______ known about the shortcut, I would have arrived on time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was", "would have", "have", "had"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$For a past condition that didn't happen, use "If I had known... I would have."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$Lightning can heat the air around it to about 30,000 degrees Celsius. ______ a single bolt is several times hotter than the surface of the sun.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Meanwhile,", "In other words,", "Instead,", "However,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence restates the first in a more vivid way: "In other words."$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$The museum's collection includes paintings from many periods. ______ it owns works from the Renaissance, the 1800s and the present day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,", "For example,", "Finally,", "Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence gives specific examples of the many periods.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$The first version of the app was slow and hard to use. ______ the designers rebuilt it from scratch, and the new version was praised for its speed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,", "In addition,", "Similarly,", "Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Rebuilding the app was a result of the first version's problems.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$Most bats eat insects. Vampire bats, ______ feed on the blood of other animals.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["by contrast,", "as a result,", "for example,", "likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Vampire bats differ from most bats, so a contrast word fits.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$To make the bread, first mix the flour, water and yeast. ______ let the dough rest for an hour so it can rise.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["On the other hand,", "In conclusion,", "Next,", "However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text gives steps in order, so a sequence word fits.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$The trail is steep and rocky in many places. ______ hikers of all ages complete it every year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,", "Specifically,", "In other words,", "Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Many people complete it despite the difficulty, which is a contrast with the first sentence.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$Electric cars produce no tailpipe emissions. ______ they are much quieter than gas-powered cars, which can reduce noise in cities.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Previously,", "Nonetheless,", "Additionally,", "Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence adds another advantage.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$The scientist expected the plants under blue light to grow the fastest. ______ the plants under red light grew taller in every trial.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,", "For this reason,", "Likewise,", "As expected,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The result went against the expectation, so "Surprisingly" fits.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$Dogs were domesticated from wolves at least 15,000 years ago. Cats, ______ were domesticated much later, probably after people began farming and storing grain.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["for example,", "on the other hand,", "as a result,", "in other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Cats are contrasted with dogs (much later).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$The city planted 5,000 trees along its busiest streets. ______ summer temperatures on those streets dropped by several degrees.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Afterward,", "In contrast,", "Even so,", "For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The temperature drop came after the planting, so a time/result transition fits.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• Wangari Maathai was a Kenyan environmental activist.
• In 1977 she founded the Green Belt Movement.
• The movement has planted tens of millions of trees in Kenya.
• In 2004 she became the first African woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the scale of the Green Belt Movement's work. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 2004, Wangari Maathai won the Nobel Peace Prize.", "Wangari Maathai was a Kenyan environmental activist.", "The Green Belt Movement, founded by Wangari Maathai in 1977, has planted tens of millions of trees in Kenya.", "Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Tens of millions of trees" shows the scale of the work.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• Venus and Earth are similar in size.
• Venus's surface temperature is about 465°C.
• Earth's average surface temperature is about 15°C.
• Venus has a thick atmosphere mostly made of carbon dioxide.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast the surface temperatures of Venus and Earth. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Venus and Earth are similar in size.", "Venus has a thick atmosphere made mostly of carbon dioxide.", "Venus's surface is about 465°C, while Earth's average surface temperature is only about 15°C.", "Earth's average surface temperature is about 15°C."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only this choice gives both temperatures side by side.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• Bessie Coleman was born in Texas in 1892.
• U.S. flight schools would not admit her because she was Black and a woman.
• She learned French and earned her pilot's license in France in 1921.
• She became the first African American woman to hold a pilot's license.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Coleman went to France to become a pilot. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because U.S. flight schools would not admit her, Bessie Coleman learned French and earned her pilot's license in France.", "Bessie Coleman earned her pilot's license in 1921.", "Bessie Coleman was the first African American woman to hold a pilot's license.", "Bessie Coleman was born in Texas in 1892."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal is the reason she went to France: U.S. schools refused her.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• Sea otters eat sea urchins.
• Sea urchins eat kelp.
• Where sea otters disappeared, urchin numbers grew and kelp forests shrank.
• Kelp forests provide habitat for many fish.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how sea otters help kelp forests. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sea otters eat sea urchins, and kelp forests are home to fish.", "Sea urchins eat kelp.", "By eating sea urchins, which eat kelp, sea otters keep urchin numbers down and help kelp forests survive.", "Kelp forests provide habitat for many fish."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The answer must connect otters to kelp through urchins.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries.
• Its total length, including all branches, is about 21,000 kilometers.
• Much of the wall that tourists visit today was built during the Ming dynasty (1368-1644).
• The wall was built mainly for defense.$q$, $q$The student wants to tell readers when the most-visited parts of the wall were built. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Great Wall of China is about 21,000 kilometers long.", "The Great Wall was built over many centuries, mainly for defense.", "The Great Wall of China was built for defense.", "Much of the Great Wall that tourists visit today was built during the Ming dynasty, between 1368 and 1644."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only this choice tells when the parts tourists visit were built.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• Study A: students who slept 8 or more hours scored an average of 84 on a math test.
• Students who slept fewer than 6 hours scored an average of 71.
• The study included 400 high school students.
• Researchers asked students to record their sleep for a week before the test.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the study's main finding. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers asked 400 high school students to record their sleep for a week.", "In a study of 400 high school students, those who slept 8 or more hours averaged 84 on a math test, compared with 71 for those who slept fewer than 6 hours.", "Students who slept fewer than 6 hours scored an average of 71.", "The study included 400 high school students."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The main finding is the comparison between the two sleep groups.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Reading and Writing$q$, $q$Rhetorical synthesis$q$, 'mc', $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken these notes:
• The okapi lives in the rainforests of central Africa.
• It has striped legs like a zebra.
• Its closest living relative is the giraffe.
• Scientists outside Africa did not describe it until 1901.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize a surprising fact about the okapi's family. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although its striped legs look like a zebra's, the okapi's closest living relative is the giraffe.", "Scientists outside Africa first described the okapi in 1901.", "The okapi has striped legs.", "The okapi lives in the rainforests of central Africa."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Contrasting zebra-like stripes with a giraffe relative highlights the surprise.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If 4x − 9 = 19, what is the value of 2x?$q$, $q$["5", "7", "14", "28"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Add 9: 4x = 28, so x = 7 and 2x = 14. Read what the question asks for: 2x, not x.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A phone plan costs $25 a month plus $0.10 for each text message over 500. Which function gives the monthly cost C, in dollars, for t text messages, where t > 500?$q$, $q$["C = 0.10(t + 500) + 25", "C = 25t + 0.10", "C = 25 + 0.10(t − 500)", "C = 25 + 0.10t"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only messages over 500 cost extra, so the extra count is t − 500, times $0.10, plus the $25 base.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Line ℓ passes through the points (0, 3) and (2, 11). Which equation represents line ℓ?$q$, $q$["y = 4x + 3", "y = 3x + 4", "y = 4x + 11", "y = 8x + 3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (11 − 3) / (2 − 0) = 4. The line crosses the y-axis at (0, 3), so b = 3.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If x + y = 10 and x − y = 4, what is the value of xy?$q$, $q$["7", "21", "24", "40"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Add the equations: 2x = 14, so x = 7 and y = 3. Then xy = 21.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear inequalities$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Maria has $60 to spend on an $18 ticket and snacks that cost $3.50 each. Which inequality represents the number of snacks, s, she can buy?$q$, $q$["3.5s ≤ 18 + 60", "18 + 3.5s ≥ 60", "18 + 3.5s ≤ 60", "18s + 3.5 ≤ 60"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ticket plus snacks must be at most $60: 18 + 3.5s ≤ 60.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$For what value of k does the system 2x + 3y = 7 and 4x + ky = 5 have no solution?$q$, $q$["−6", "3", "6", "14"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$No solution means parallel lines: same slope, different intercept. Doubling the first equation gives 4x + 6y = 14, so k = 6 makes the left sides match while 14 ≠ 5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What are the solutions of x² − 5x − 14 = 0?$q$, $q$["x = 5 and x = −14", "x = −7 and x = 2", "x = 14 and x = −1", "x = 7 and x = −2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Factor: (x − 7)(x + 2) = 0, so x = 7 or x = −2. Check: 7 × −2 = −14 and 7 + (−2) = 5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If f(x) = x² − 3x + 2, what is f(−2)?$q$, $q$["−8", "0", "4", "12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$(−2)² − 3(−2) + 2 = 4 + 6 + 2 = 12. Watch the signs: minus a negative is plus.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponential models$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A town's population of 8,000 grows by 3% each year. Which expression gives the population after t years?$q$, $q$["8,000(1.03)^t", "8,000(0.03)^t", "8,000 + 1.03t", "8,000(1.3)^t"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Growing by 3% means multiplying by 1 + 0.03 = 1.03 each year.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x + 4)²?$q$, $q$["x² + 4x + 16", "x² + 16", "x² + 8x + 8", "x² + 8x + 16"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$(x + 4)(x + 4) = x² + 4x + 4x + 16 = x² + 8x + 16. Don't forget the middle term.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If 3^(x + 1) = 81, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["2", "3", "4", "27"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$81 = 3⁴, so x + 1 = 4 and x = 3.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 'mc', null, $q$The function g is defined by g(x) = 2^x. What is the value of g(5) − g(3)?$q$, $q$["2", "4", "16", "24"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$g(5) = 32 and g(3) = 8, so the difference is 24. It isn't 2^(5 − 3).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$The graph of a quadratic function is a parabola with its vertex at (2, −5) that opens upward. What is the minimum value of the function?$q$, $q$["−5", "−2", "2", "5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An upward parabola's lowest point is its vertex. The minimum value is the y-coordinate: −5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (6x³ − 9x²) / (3x), for x ≠ 0?$q$, $q$["3x² − 6x", "2x² − 9x²", "2x³ − 3x²", "2x² − 3x"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Divide each term by 3x: 6x³ / 3x = 2x² and 9x² / 3x = 3x.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Radical equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If √(x + 5) = 4, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["−1", "3", "11", "21"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Square both sides: x + 5 = 16, so x = 11. Check: √16 = 4.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Ratios and rates$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A recipe uses 3 cups of flour for every 2 cups of sugar. How many cups of sugar are needed with 12 cups of flour?$q$, $q$["4", "6", "8", "18"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$12 is 4 times 3, so use 4 times 2 = 8 cups of sugar.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A jacket that normally costs $80 is on sale for 25% off. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$20", "$55", "$60", "$75"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$25% of $80 is $20, and $80 − $20 = $60. ($20 is the discount, not the price.)$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Statistics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$The mean of five numbers is 12. Four of the numbers are 10, 14, 9 and 15. What is the fifth number?$q$, $q$["10", "12", "13", "48"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The five numbers add to 5 × 12 = 60. The four given add to 48, so the fifth is 12.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Statistics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the median of the data set 2, 4, 6, 9, 10, 13?$q$, $q$["About 7.3", "6", "7.5", "9"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$With six numbers, the median is the average of the middle two: (6 + 9) / 2 = 7.5. About 7.3 is the mean.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Sampling$q$, 'mc', null, $q$In a random sample of 400 voters in a city, 240 support building a new park. Based on the sample, which is the best estimate of how many of the city's 50,000 voters support the park?$q$, $q$["240", "20,000", "24,000", "30,000"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$240 / 400 = 60%, and 60% of 50,000 is 30,000.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A bag holds 5 red, 3 blue and 2 green marbles. One marble is drawn at random. What is the probability that it is not red?$q$, $q$["1/2", "2/5", "1/5", "3/10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5 of the 10 marbles are not red (3 blue + 2 green), so 5/10 = 1/2.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Ratios and rates$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A car travels 150 miles in 2.5 hours at a constant speed. At this speed, how far will it travel in 4 hours?$q$, $q$["240 miles", "200 miles", "160 miles", "375 miles"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Speed = 150 / 2.5 = 60 miles per hour, and 60 × 4 = 240 miles.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Area and perimeter$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A rectangle has a perimeter of 30 and a length of 9. What is its area?$q$, $q$["21", "45", "54", "270"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$2(9) + 2w = 30, so w = 6. Area = 9 × 6 = 54.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 'mc', null, $q$In a right triangle, one acute angle measures 35°. Which expression is equal to sin(55°)?$q$, $q$["tan(35°)", "1 − sin(35°)", "sin(35°)", "cos(35°)"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The two acute angles add to 90°. The sine of one acute angle equals the cosine of the other.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane has the equation (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25. What are its center and radius?$q$, $q$["Center (−3, 2), radius 25", "Center (−3, 2), radius 5", "Center (3, −2), radius 5", "Center (3, −2), radius 25"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r², the center is (h, k) = (3, −2) and r² = 25, so r = 5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Similar figures$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Two similar triangles have corresponding sides of length 4 and 10. If the smaller triangle's area is 12, what is the larger triangle's area?$q$, $q$["30", "60", "75", "150"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The side ratio is 10/4 = 2.5, so the area ratio is 2.5² = 6.25. 12 × 6.25 = 75.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Volume$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A cylinder has a radius of 3 and a height of 10. What is its volume?$q$, $q$["90π", "60π", "900π", "30π"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$V = πr²h = π × 9 × 10 = 90π.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A right triangle has legs of length 5 and 12. What is the cosine of the angle opposite the side of length 5?$q$, $q$["13/12", "5/13", "12/13", "5/12"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is 13 (5-12-13). For the angle opposite 5, the adjacent leg is 12, so cos = 12/13.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If 7x + 5 = 3x + 29, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["3.4", "6", "8.5", "24"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Subtract 3x: 4x + 5 = 29. Subtract 5: 4x = 24, so x = 6.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If (x / 3) − 4 = 2, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["−6", "2", "6", "18"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Add 4: x / 3 = 6. Multiply by 3: x = 18.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If 2(3x − 1) = 4x + 10, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["2", "4.5", "6", "12"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$6x − 2 = 4x + 10, so 2x = 12 and x = 6.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If 5a − 2 = 18, what is the value of 10a − 4?$q$, $q$["4", "18", "36", "40"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$10a − 4 is exactly 2 × (5a − 2) = 2 × 18 = 36. No need to find a.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$How many solutions does the equation 3(x + 2) = 3x + 6 have?$q$, $q$["Infinitely many", "Exactly one", "None", "Exactly two"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both sides simplify to 3x + 6, so every x works.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$How many solutions does the equation 4x − 7 = 4(x − 2) have?$q$, $q$["None", "Exactly two", "Exactly one", "Infinitely many"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The right side is 4x − 8. Then 4x − 7 = 4x − 8 gives −7 = −8, which is never true.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A plumber charges a $45 visit fee plus $30 per hour. A bill came to $165. How many hours did the plumber work?$q$, $q$["3", "4", "5.5", "7"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$45 + 30h = 165, so 30h = 120 and h = 4.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = 5x − 3. What is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["2", "12", "17", "23"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$f(4) = 5(4) − 3 = 20 − 3 = 17.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A linear function has f(0) = 7 and f(2) = 13. What is f(5)?$q$, $q$["20", "22", "25", "35"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slope = (13 − 7) / 2 = 3, so f(x) = 3x + 7 and f(5) = 22.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A candle is 30 cm tall and burns down 1.5 cm per hour. Which equation gives its height h, in cm, after t hours?$q$, $q$["h = 30 + 1.5t", "h = 30t − 1.5", "h = 30 − 1.5t", "h = 1.5 − 30t"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$It starts at 30 and loses 1.5 each hour: h = 30 − 1.5t.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$The equation C = 12n + 40 gives the cost C, in dollars, to rent a party room for n guests. What does 12 represent?$q$, $q$["The total cost for 12 guests", "The cost of the room with no guests", "The additional cost for each guest", "The number of guests allowed"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$12 is multiplied by n (guests), so it's the cost per guest. 40 is the fixed cost.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$In the equation y = 0.8x + 15, y is a tree's height in feet and x is the number of years after it was planted. What is the best interpretation of 15?$q$, $q$["The number of feet the tree grows each year", "The number of years until the tree is 0.8 feet tall", "The tree's height after 15 years", "The tree's height when it was planted"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$When x = 0 (planting), y = 15. So 15 is the starting height.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Lines$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the slope of a line perpendicular to y = (2/3)x + 1?$q$, $q$["2/3", "−2/3", "−3/2", "3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals: −1 ÷ (2/3) = −3/2.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Lines$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which equation is a line parallel to y = −4x + 2 that passes through (0, −5)?$q$, $q$["y = −4x − 5", "y = −4x + 5", "y = 4x − 5", "y = (1/4)x − 5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Parallel lines share the slope −4; passing through (0, −5) makes the intercept −5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Lines$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A line passes through (1, 4) and (3, 10). What is its y-intercept?$q$, $q$["−2", "1", "3", "4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slope = (10 − 4) / (3 − 1) = 3. Then 4 = 3(1) + b, so b = 1.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Lines$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line 3x + 4y = 24?$q$, $q$["4", "6", "8", "24"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Set y = 0: 3x = 24, so x = 8.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["3", "4", "5", "6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Add the equations: 3x = 12, so x = 4 (and y = 3).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If y = 3x − 2 and y = x + 6, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["4", "8", "10", "12"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Set 3x − 2 = x + 6, so x = 4. Then y = 4 + 6 = 10.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Tickets to a school play cost $5 for students and $8 for adults. For one show, 120 tickets were sold for $750. How many student tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["50", "60", "70", "90"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$s + a = 120 and 5s + 8a = 750. Replace a with 120 − s: 5s + 960 − 8s = 750, so 3s = 210 and s = 70.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$For what value of c does the system x + 2y = 5 and 3x + 6y = c have infinitely many solutions?$q$, $q$["3", "5", "10", "15"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Multiplying the first equation by 3 gives 3x + 6y = 15. The equations are the same line when c = 15.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If 3a + 2b = 16 and a + 2b = 8, what is the value of a?$q$, $q$["2", "4", "6", "8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Subtract the second equation from the first: 2a = 8, so a = 4.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A store sells pens for $2 each and notebooks for $5 each. Jada bought 9 items for $30. How many notebooks did she buy?$q$, $q$["3", "4", "5", "6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$p + n = 9 and 2p + 5n = 30. Then 2(9 − n) + 5n = 30, so 3n = 12 and n = 4.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear inequalities$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which value of x is a solution to 3x − 4 > 11?$q$, $q$["0", "3", "5", "6"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$3x > 15, so x > 5. Only 6 is greater than 5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear inequalities$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A delivery van can carry at most 2,000 pounds. The driver weighs 180 pounds and each box weighs 40 pounds. What is the greatest number of boxes the van can carry with the driver?$q$, $q$["44", "45", "46", "50"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$180 + 40b ≤ 2,000, so 40b ≤ 1,820 and b ≤ 45.5. The greatest whole number is 45.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear inequalities$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which inequality describes all solutions of −2x + 5 ≤ 13?$q$, $q$["x ≤ −4", "x ≥ −4", "x ≤ 9", "x ≥ 4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$−2x ≤ 8. Dividing by −2 flips the sign: x ≥ −4.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear inequalities$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Sam earns $15 an hour and wants to earn more than $400 this month. Which inequality shows the hours h he must work?$q$, $q$["15h < 400", "400h > 15", "15h > 400", "h + 15 > 400"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Pay is 15h, and it must be more than 400.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$The sum of three consecutive integers is 72. What is the largest of the three?$q$, $q$["23", "24", "25", "26"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$n + (n + 1) + (n + 2) = 72, so 3n = 69 and n = 23. The largest is 25.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If (2x + 6) / 4 = 5, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4", "7", "8", "13"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply by 4: 2x + 6 = 20, so 2x = 14 and x = 7.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A gym charges a $50 sign-up fee and $25 per month. After how many months will the total paid reach $300?$q$, $q$["8", "10", "12", "14"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$50 + 25m = 300, so 25m = 250 and m = 10.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the positive solution of x² + 3x − 28 = 0?$q$, $q$["3", "4", "7", "28"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Factor: (x + 7)(x − 4) = 0, so x = −7 or 4. The positive solution is 4.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What are the solutions of 2x² − 18 = 0?$q$, $q$["x = 9 and x = −9", "x = 6 and x = −6", "x = 3 and x = −3", "x = 3 only"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$2x² = 18, so x² = 9 and x = ±3.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What are the x-intercepts of the graph of y = (x − 5)(x + 1)?$q$, $q$["(5, 0) and (−1, 0)", "(−5, 0) and (1, 0)", "(5, 0) and (1, 0)", "(0, 5) and (0, −1)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$y = 0 when x − 5 = 0 or x + 1 = 0, so x = 5 or x = −1.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the vertex of the parabola y = (x − 3)² + 4?$q$, $q$["(3, −4)", "(4, 3)", "(−3, 4)", "(3, 4)"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form y = (x − h)² + k, the vertex is (h, k) = (3, 4).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$How many real solutions does x² + 4x + 9 = 0 have?$q$, $q$["Infinitely many", "None", "One", "Two"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The discriminant is b² − 4ac = 16 − 36 = −20, which is negative, so there are no real solutions.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$For what value of k does x² − 10x + k = 0 have exactly one real solution?$q$, $q$["5", "10", "25", "100"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$One solution means b² − 4ac = 0: 100 − 4k = 0, so k = 25. (Then x² − 10x + 25 = (x − 5)².)$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A ball's height, in feet, t seconds after it is thrown is h(t) = −16t² + 64t. After how many seconds does it hit the ground?$q$, $q$["2", "4", "8", "16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$h(t) = −16t(t − 4) = 0 when t = 0 (thrown) or t = 4 (lands).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the maximum height of the ball in h(t) = −16t² + 64t, in feet?$q$, $q$["32", "48", "64", "128"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The peak is halfway between the roots 0 and 4, at t = 2: h(2) = −64 + 128 = 64.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the sum of the solutions of x² − 7x + 10 = 0?$q$, $q$["−7", "3", "7", "10"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Factor: (x − 2)(x − 5) = 0, so x = 2 and 5, and 2 + 5 = 7.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (3x − 2)(x + 5)?$q$, $q$["3x² − 13x − 10", "3x² + 15x − 10", "3x² + 13x − 10", "3x² + 13x + 10"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$3x·x = 3x², 3x·5 = 15x, −2·x = −2x, −2·5 = −10. Combine: 3x² + 13x − 10.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to x² − 49?$q$, $q$["(x − 7)²", "(x + 7)²", "(x − 49)(x + 1)", "(x − 7)(x + 7)"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A difference of squares: a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to 4x² − 12x?$q$, $q$["2x(2x − 12)", "4x(x − 3)", "4x(x − 12)", "4(x² − 12x)"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Factor out 4x: 4x² − 12x = 4x(x − 3).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x² + 5x + 6) / (x + 2), for x ≠ −2?$q$, $q$["x² + 3", "x + 3", "x + 2", "x + 6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$x² + 5x + 6 = (x + 2)(x + 3); cancel (x + 2) to get x + 3.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to x⁵ · x³?$q$, $q$["x²", "x¹⁵", "2x⁸", "x⁸"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents: 5 + 3 = 8.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x⁶) / (x²), for x ≠ 0?$q$, $q$["x⁴", "x³", "x⁸", "x¹²"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When dividing powers with the same base, subtract the exponents: 6 − 2 = 4.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the value of 16^(1/2) + 8^(2/3)?$q$, $q$["6", "8", "12", "20"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$16^(1/2) = 4 and 8^(2/3) = (∛8)² = 2² = 4, so the sum is 8.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to x^(3/4)?$q$, $q$["∛(x⁴)", "x³ / 4", "⁴√(x³)", "3x / 4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$x^(a/b) = the bth root of x^a, so x^(3/4) = ⁴√(x³).$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponential models$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A car worth $24,000 loses 15% of its value each year. Which expression gives its value after t years?$q$, $q$["24,000(0.85)^t", "24,000 − 0.15t", "24,000(0.15)^t", "24,000(1.15)^t"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Losing 15% means keeping 85% each year: multiply by 0.85.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponential models$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A bacteria colony doubles every 3 hours. It starts with 500 bacteria. How many are there after 12 hours?$q$, $q$["2,000", "4,000", "6,000", "8,000"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$12 hours is 4 doublings: 500 × 2⁴ = 500 × 16 = 8,000.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponential models$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Which function shows exponential growth?$q$, $q$["f(x) = 3(1.2)^x", "f(x) = 3(0.8)^x", "f(x) = 3x²", "f(x) = 1.2x + 3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Exponential growth has the form a·b^x with b > 1. 0.8 is decay; the others aren't exponential.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Exponential models$q$, 'mc', null, $q$An account earns 4% interest compounded yearly. Starting with $1,000, about how much is in the account after 2 years?$q$, $q$["$1,040.00", "$1,080.00", "$1,081.60", "$1,160.00"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$1,000 × 1.04² = 1,000 × 1.0816 = $1,081.60.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If g(x) = x² + 1, what is g(a + 1)?$q$, $q$["a² + 2a + 1", "a² + 1", "a² + 2", "a² + 2a + 2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$(a + 1)² + 1 = a² + 2a + 1 + 1 = a² + 2a + 2.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x − 4, what is f(g(6))?$q$, $q$["−3", "5", "9", "13"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$g(6) = 2, then f(2) = 2(2) + 1 = 5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Functions$q$, 'mc', null, $q$The function h(x) = (x − 2)² is shifted 3 units up. Which equation is the new function?$q$, $q$["y = (x − 2)² + 3", "y = (x − 5)²", "y = 3(x − 2)²", "y = (x + 1)²"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Shifting up adds 3 to the output: (x − 2)² + 3.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Radical equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the solution of √(2x − 1) = 5?$q$, $q$["3", "12", "13", "26"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Square both sides: 2x − 1 = 25, so 2x = 26 and x = 13.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Rational equations$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the solution of 6 / (x − 1) = 2?$q$, $q$["2", "3", "4", "7"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply by (x − 1): 6 = 2(x − 1) = 2x − 2, so 2x = 8 and x = 4.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A store raises the price of a $50 item by 20%, then lowers the new price by 20%. What is the final price?$q$, $q$["$40", "$48", "$50", "$52"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$50 × 1.2 = 60, then 60 × 0.8 = 48. A 20% cut of a bigger number is more than the 20% raise.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 'mc', null, $q$In a class of 32 students, 24 passed a test. What percent of the class passed?$q$, $q$["24%", "70%", "75%", "80%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$24 / 32 = 0.75 = 75%.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Ratios and rates$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A map uses a scale of 1 inch = 25 miles. Two towns are 3.4 inches apart on the map. How far apart are they?$q$, $q$["28.4 miles", "75 miles", "100 miles", "85 miles"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$3.4 × 25 = 85 miles.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Ratios and rates$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A printer prints 18 pages in 45 seconds. At this rate, how many pages does it print in 5 minutes?$q$, $q$["90", "100", "120", "150"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$18 / 45 = 0.4 pages per second. 5 minutes = 300 seconds, and 0.4 × 300 = 120.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Statistics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Data set A is 10, 10, 10, 10, 10. Data set B is 2, 6, 10, 14, 18. Which statement is true?$q$, $q$["A has a larger standard deviation.", "The sets have different means.", "The sets have the same mean, but B has a larger standard deviation.", "The sets have the same standard deviation."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both means are 10. B's values are more spread out, so its standard deviation is larger.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Statistics$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A data set's largest value is changed from 40 to 90. Which statistic is most likely to change by the most?$q$, $q$["The mode", "The mean", "The median", "The number of values"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The mean uses every value, so a big outlier pulls it. The median depends only on the middle.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 'mc', null, $q$In a survey, 60 students chose soccer, 45 chose basketball and 15 chose tennis as their favorite sport. If one of these students is chosen at random, what is the probability the student chose basketball?$q$, $q$["1/3", "1/8", "3/8", "3/4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$There are 60 + 45 + 15 = 120 students; 45 / 120 = 3/8.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Sampling$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A researcher wants to know how much screen time teenagers in a city have. Which sample would give the most reliable estimate?$q$, $q$["300 members of a city video-game club", "300 teens chosen at random from all city high schools", "Every teen who answers an online poll", "30 teens at one school's library"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A random sample from the whole population avoids bias. The others select teens who may differ from typical teens.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Angles$q$, 'mc', null, $q$Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle measures 68°. What is the measure of an angle that forms a straight line with it?$q$, $q$["122°", "112°", "68°", "22°"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Angles that form a straight line add to 180°: 180 − 68 = 112°.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Triangles$q$, 'mc', null, $q$In isosceles triangle ABC, AB = AC and angle A measures 40°. What is the measure of angle B?$q$, $q$["40°", "80°", "70°", "140°"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The base angles are equal and share 180 − 40 = 140°, so each is 70°.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A 13-foot ladder leans against a wall with its base 5 feet from the wall. How high up the wall does it reach?$q$, $q$["8 feet", "12 feet", "√194 feet", "18 feet"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$5² + h² = 13², so h² = 169 − 25 = 144 and h = 12.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 'mc', null, $q$In a 30°-60°-90° triangle, the shortest side is 6. How long is the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["6√2", "6√3", "12", "9"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In a 30-60-90 triangle the hypotenuse is twice the shortest side: 12.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 'mc', null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is 90°, PR = 9 and PQ = 15. What is sin Q?$q$, $q$["3/5", "5/3", "3/4", "4/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Side PR is opposite angle Q, and PQ is the hypotenuse: sin Q = 9 / 15 = 3/5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A circle has a radius of 10. What is the length of an arc with a central angle of 72°?$q$, $q$["8π", "20π", "2π", "4π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Arc length = (72/360) × 2π(10) = (1/5) × 20π = 4π.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 'mc', null, $q$What is the radius of the circle x² + y² − 6x + 8y = 0?$q$, $q$["25", "√14", "5", "10"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Complete the square: (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 9 + 16 = 25, so r = 5.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Volume$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A cube has a volume of 64 cubic inches. What is its surface area?$q$, $q$["64 square inches", "16 square inches", "96 square inches", "384 square inches"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Each edge is ∛64 = 4, each face is 16, and there are 6 faces: 96.$q$),
  ('SAT', $q$SAT Math$q$, $q$Area and perimeter$q$, 'mc', null, $q$A rectangle is 3 times as long as it is wide, and its perimeter is 48. What is its area?$q$, $q$["36", "96", "108", "144"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$2(3w) + 2w = 48, so 8w = 48 and w = 6, length 18. Area = 6 × 18 = 108.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Verb tense$q$, 'mc', $q$My grandmother kept a garden behind her house in Macon. Every summer [[she grows]] tomatoes, peppers and okra.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "she will grow", "she has been growing", "she grew"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The paragraph is in the past ("kept"), so use the past tense: "she grew."$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Punctuation$q$, 'mc', $q$The garden was small, [[but it]] produced enough vegetables to feed our whole family.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "it", "but it,", "but, it"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Comma plus "but" correctly joins two complete sentences. The other choices add a wrong comma or create a comma splice.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Punctuation$q$, 'mc', $q$We spent hours [[picking, washing and, sorting]] the vegetables.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "picking; washing; and sorting", "picking washing, and sorting", "picking, washing, and sorting"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Separate items in a list with commas, and don't put a comma after "and."$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Agreement$q$, 'mc', $q$Each of the volunteers [[were given]] a map of the trail.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "were giving", "was given", "have been given"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "Each" (singular), so the verb is "was given."$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Concision$q$, 'mc', $q$At 5 a.m. [[in the early morning]], the bakers begin mixing dough.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "in the morning hours", "early in the morning", "DELETE the underlined portion."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"5 a.m." already says it's early morning, so the phrase is redundant. Delete it.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Usage$q$, 'mc', $q$The company updated [[it's]] website last week.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "its'", "its", "their"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The website belongs to the company: possessive "its." "It's" means "it is," and "its'" is never correct.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Sentence structure$q$, 'mc', $q$The storm knocked out power across the [[county, crews]] worked through the night to restore it.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "county crews", "county, and, crews", "county; crews"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two complete sentences can be joined with a semicolon. A comma alone is a comma splice.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 'mc', $q$Our team practiced every afternoon for a month. [[Nevertheless,]] we won the regional tournament.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "In contrast,", "Otherwise,", "As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Winning follows from practicing, so a cause-and-effect transition fits. "Nevertheless" signals a surprise.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Concision$q$, 'mc', $q$The new law [[will have an effect on and impact]] how schools plan their budgets.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "will effect", "will have affect on", "will affect"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Will affect" says the same thing in two words. "Affect" is the verb; "effect" is usually the noun.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Writing strategy$q$, 'mc', $q$The Georgia Aquarium in Atlanta opened in 2005. It houses thousands of animals, including whale sharks. The writer is considering adding this sentence: "My cousin got a job at a pizza place near the aquarium last year."$q$, $q$Should the writer make this addition?$q$, $q$["Yes, because it shows that the aquarium is in a busy area.", "No, because it repeats information given earlier.", "No, because it is unrelated to the paragraph's focus on the aquarium.", "Yes, because it adds a personal detail that keeps readers interested."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The paragraph is about the aquarium and its animals. A cousin's pizza job is off topic.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Usage$q$, 'mc', $q$The band members tuned [[there]] instruments before the concert.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "they're", "its", "their"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The instruments belong to the band members: "their." "There" is a place; "they're" means "they are."$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Usage$q$, 'mc', $q$Of the two routes, the river trail is the [[most scenic]].$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "more scenic", "most scenic of all", "scenicest"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Comparing two things uses "more"; "most" is for three or more.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Modifiers$q$, 'mc', $q$Covered in mud, [[the dog ran]] into the kitchen after the storm.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "the dog's running went", "running happened by the dog", "the kitchen saw the dog run"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Covered in mud" describes the dog, so "the dog" must come right after the comma. The original is correct.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Usage$q$, 'mc', $q$The coach, [[who]] had played in college, knew every drill.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "whom", "which", "whose"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Who" is the subject of "had played," so "who" is correct. Use "which" for things, not people.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Punctuation$q$, 'mc', $q$Both of my [[sister's]] cars need new tires.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "sisters's", "sisters", "sisters'"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Both" means two sisters, so use the plural possessive: "sisters'."$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Sentence structure$q$, 'mc', $q$On weekends she likes hiking, swimming and [[to ride]] her bike.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "ride", "riding", "she rides"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Items in a list should match: hiking, swimming and riding.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Writing strategy$q$, 'mc', $q$By 8 a.m. the farmers market was [[busy]]. The writer wants to add a detail that best shows how crowded the market was.$q$, $q$Which choice best accomplishes this goal?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "so packed that shoppers had to turn sideways to pass between the stalls", "open for business", "a place that many people like to visit"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Turning sideways to squeeze between stalls is a specific picture of a crowd. The others are vague or off goal.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Agreement$q$, 'mc', $q$Neither the coach nor the players [[was]] ready for the rain.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "is", "were", "has been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$With "neither... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "players" (plural): "were."$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Punctuation$q$, 'mc', $q$She packed everything she needed for the [[trip: a]] tent, a stove and a sleeping bag.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "trip. A", "trip; a", "trip, which was a"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon after a complete sentence introduces a list. The original is correct.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT English$q$, $q$Concision$q$, 'mc', $q$The reason the meeting was canceled [[is because]] the speaker was sick.$q$, $q$Which choice is best?$q$, $q$["NO CHANGE", "is that", "is due to the fact that because", "was on account of because"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The reason... is because" repeats itself. "The reason... is that" is correct and clear.$q$),
