  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$Between 1883 and 1919, the industrialist Andrew Carnegie paid for the construction of more than 1,600 public library buildings in the United States. Today many Americans take the neighborhood library for granted, but in the late nineteenth century, free public libraries were rare outside large cities. Many collections belonged to private clubs or subscription libraries that charged members a fee.

Carnegie's gifts came with conditions. A town that wanted a library had to provide the land and promise to spend a set amount each year, usually about ten percent of the building grant, on books, staff and upkeep. Carnegie believed that a community should invest in its own library rather than simply receive one. Critics at the time complained that the requirement was a burden for small towns, and some communities turned the money down. Others objected to accepting money from a man whose steel company had been involved in bitter labor disputes.

Still, the program reshaped American towns. Because Carnegie paid for buildings rather than collections, local residents decided what the shelves would hold, and many libraries became gathering places for lectures, clubs and classes. Some historians argue that the libraries also helped turn librarianship into a profession, since towns needed trained staff to meet their yearly commitments.

Not every community benefited equally. In the segregated South, many Carnegie libraries were closed to Black residents, and only a small number of separate branches were built to serve them. The program's legacy, then, reflects both the promise of free public learning and the limits on who was allowed to share in it.$q$, $q$The main purpose of the passage is to:$q$, $q$["explain how a town can apply for a library grant today", "describe Carnegie's library program, including its conditions, its effects and its limits", "compare subscription libraries with university libraries", "argue that Carnegie was the most generous American in history"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Each paragraph covers one part: the program, its conditions, its effects and who was left out.$q$),
