Still, the program reshaped American towns. Because Carnegie paid for buildings rather than collections, local residents decided what the shelves would hold, and many libraries became gathering places for lectures, clubs and classes. Some historians argue that the libraries also helped turn librarianship into a profession, since towns needed trained staff to meet their yearly commitments.

Not every community benefited equally. In the segregated South, many Carnegie libraries were closed to Black residents, and only a small number of separate branches were built to serve them. The program's legacy, then, reflects both the promise of free public learning and the limits on who was allowed to share in it.$q$, $q$According to the passage, a town that accepted a Carnegie grant had to:$q$, $q$["charge residents a membership fee", "buy books that Carnegie chose", "name the library after Carnegie", "provide the land and fund the library each year"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Paragraph 2: towns had to "provide the land and promise to spend a set amount each year."$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 'mc', $q$Between 1883 and 1919, the industrialist Andrew Carnegie paid for the construction of more than 1,600 public library buildings in the United States. Today many Americans take the neighborhood library for granted, but in the late nineteenth century, free public libraries were rare outside large cities. Many collections belonged to private clubs or subscription libraries that charged members a fee.

Carnegie's gifts came with conditions. A town that wanted a library had to provide the land and promise to spend a set amount each year, usually about ten percent of the building grant, on books, staff and upkeep. Carnegie believed that a community should invest in its own library rather than simply receive one. Critics at the time complained that the requirement was a burden for small towns, and some communities turned the money down. Others objected to accepting money from a man whose steel company had been involved in bitter labor disputes.

Still, the program reshaped American towns. Because Carnegie paid for buildings rather than collections, local residents decided what the shelves would hold, and many libraries became gathering places for lectures, clubs and classes. Some historians argue that the libraries also helped turn librarianship into a profession, since towns needed trained staff to meet their yearly commitments.

Not every community benefited equally. In the segregated South, many Carnegie libraries were closed to Black residents, and only a small number of separate branches were built to serve them. The program's legacy, then, reflects both the promise of free public learning and the limits on who was allowed to share in it.$q$, $q$The passage suggests that Carnegie required a yearly commitment from towns mainly because he believed that:$q$, $q$["communities should invest in their own libraries", "the federal government should pay librarians", "small towns could not be trusted with money", "libraries should be run as private clubs"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Carnegie believed that a community should invest in its own library rather than simply receive one."$q$),
