Still, the program reshaped American towns. Because Carnegie paid for buildings rather than collections, local residents decided what the shelves would hold, and many libraries became gathering places for lectures, clubs and classes. Some historians argue that the libraries also helped turn librarianship into a profession, since towns needed trained staff to meet their yearly commitments.

Not every community benefited equally. In the segregated South, many Carnegie libraries were closed to Black residents, and only a small number of separate branches were built to serve them. The program's legacy, then, reflects both the promise of free public learning and the limits on who was allowed to share in it.$q$, $q$In the last paragraph, the author's attitude toward the program's legacy is best described as:$q$, $q$["entirely approving", "entirely critical", "uninterested", "balanced, recognizing both its benefits and its unfairness"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Both the promise of free public learning and the limits" shows the author weighs both sides.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Details$q$, 'mc', $q$Each fall, millions of monarch butterflies in eastern North America begin a journey that can stretch nearly 3,000 miles, ending in the mountain forests of central Mexico. What makes the migration remarkable is not only its distance but the fact that none of the butterflies making the trip has ever made it before.

Monarchs born in early summer typically live only a few weeks. They mate, lay eggs on milkweed plants and die, and their offspring do the same. But the generation that emerges in late summer is different. These butterflies delay reproduction and can live for eight months or more, long enough to fly south, spend the winter clustered on fir trees, and begin the return trip in spring. In some cases it is their great-grandchildren that finally reach the northern edge of the range the following summer.

How do first-time travelers find their way? Researchers have found that monarchs use the position of the sun as a compass. Because the sun moves across the sky during the day, a sun compass works only if the animal can account for the time. Experiments suggest that monarchs rely on an internal clock, and some of that timekeeping happens in their antennae. In one study, butterflies whose antennae were painted black, blocking light, lost their sense of direction, while those whose antennae were coated with clear paint flew normally.

Scientists are still working to understand how monarchs find the same small patches of forest year after year. Some suspect that the butterflies also sense Earth's magnetic field, especially on cloudy days when the sun is hidden.$q$, $q$According to the passage, the generation of monarchs that migrates south differs from earlier summer generations because it:$q$, $q$["feeds only on fir trees", "delays reproduction and lives much longer", "travels only on cloudy days", "has already made the trip once before"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Paragraph 2: the late-summer generation delays reproduction and can live eight months or more.$q$),
