  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$The term "food desert" describes a neighborhood where residents have limited access to fresh, affordable food, usually because no full-service grocery store is nearby. In the United States, the Department of Agriculture has used measures such as distance to the nearest supermarket and household income to identify these areas. In a city, a neighborhood might qualify if many residents live more than a mile from a supermarket; in rural areas, the distance used is often ten miles.

For years, the standard solution seemed simple: open a supermarket. Several cities offered tax breaks to grocery chains willing to build in underserved neighborhoods. The results were mixed. Some studies found that when a new supermarket opened, residents' diets changed less than expected. Many families continued to shop where they had before, and the healthiest foods were not always the ones people could afford or had time to cook.

These findings pushed researchers and community groups to look beyond distance. Price, transportation, cooking time, and familiarity with foods all shape what families eat. In response, some communities have tried a mix of approaches: farmers markets that accept food assistance benefits, corner stores stocked with fresh produce, cooking classes, and bus routes that stop at grocery stores.

None of these efforts alone solves the problem. But together they reflect a broader understanding: access to healthy food depends not only on where a store is but on whether people can realistically use it.$q$, $q$The main purpose of the passage is to:$q$, $q$["explain why solving food deserts has required more than building supermarkets", "compare diets in rural and urban areas", "argue that supermarkets should receive more tax breaks", "describe how the Department of Agriculture was founded"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage moves from the simple fix (a supermarket) to why it fell short and what else communities try.$q$),
