  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Structure$q$, 'mc', $q$The term "food desert" describes a neighborhood where residents have limited access to fresh, affordable food, usually because no full-service grocery store is nearby. In the United States, the Department of Agriculture has used measures such as distance to the nearest supermarket and household income to identify these areas. In a city, a neighborhood might qualify if many residents live more than a mile from a supermarket; in rural areas, the distance used is often ten miles.

