  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Details$q$, 'mc', $q$Tides rise and fall along most coastlines about twice a day, and the main cause is the Moon. Gravity pulls on everything, but its strength weakens with distance. The side of Earth facing the Moon is pulled slightly more strongly than Earth's center, and Earth's center is pulled more strongly than the far side. These differences stretch Earth's oceans into two bulges: one facing the Moon and one on the opposite side. As Earth rotates through both bulges each day, most places experience two high tides and two low tides.

The Sun also affects tides, though less than the Moon does. The Sun is far more massive, but it is also much farther away, and the difference in its pull from one side of Earth to the other is smaller. When the Sun, Moon and Earth line up, at new moon and full moon, their effects combine to produce especially large "spring tides." (The name has nothing to do with the season.) When the Sun and Moon are at right angles to each other, during the quarter moons, their effects partly cancel, producing smaller "neap tides."

Local geography matters too. The shape of a bay or the slope of the sea floor can make tides much larger in some places than others. In a few narrow bays, the difference between high and low tide can exceed ten meters, while some enclosed seas have tides of only a few centimeters.$q$, $q$According to the passage, most places experience two high tides a day because:$q$, $q$["the Moon orbits Earth twice a day", "Earth rotates through two bulges of ocean water", "the Sun and Moon take turns pulling on the ocean", "winds push water toward the shore twice daily"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Paragraph 1: Earth rotates through both bulges each day.$q$),
