  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$Grandma Ruth did not believe in recipes. "A recipe," she told me the first summer I stayed with her, "is just somebody else's guess." She cooked by smell and by the sound of the pan, and when I asked how much flour went into her biscuits, she held up her hand, cupped, as though the answer were obvious.

I was fifteen and had come to her farmhouse in south Georgia because my parents were moving across the country and needed me out of the way for two months. I had expected to be bored. Instead I was put to work. By the second week I could tell, from across the kitchen, when the onions had gone from soft to sweet.

Near the end of the summer I decided to write her biscuits down. I followed her around the kitchen with a notebook and a set of measuring cups, catching handfuls of flour before they reached the bowl. She let me, though she rolled her eyes. When I finished, I baked a batch from my notes. They were fine. They were not hers.

"You measured everything," she said, tasting one, "except the part that matters." I asked what that was. She thought for a while. "Paying attention," she said.

I still have the notebook. The recipe is in it, every cup and spoon. In the margin, in my fifteen-year-old handwriting, I wrote one more line after she said that: Listen to the pan.

(Passage written for ScholarTasks practice.)$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["a contest to bake the best biscuits", "a grandmother's plan to sell her farm", "a family's move across the country", "a teenager learning from her grandmother that good cooking depends on attention, not just measurements"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The narrator measures the recipe but learns the missing piece is paying attention.$q$),
