  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$In the 1930s, during the Great Depression, the U.S. government hired thousands of out-of-work writers through a program called the Federal Writers' Project. Among their assignments was one that historians now consider priceless: interviewing elderly Americans who had been born into slavery.

Between 1936 and 1938, interviewers recorded the memories of more than two thousand formerly enslaved people across seventeen states. The interviewees described daily work, family life, punishment, escape, and the moment they learned they were free. Many were children when the Civil War ended, so their accounts reflect a child's view of slavery and its end.

Historians use these narratives carefully. Most interviewers were white, and in the segregated South, some interviewees may not have spoken freely to them. Interviewers sometimes wrote down speech in exaggerated dialect, and some edited the stories they collected. The interviews also took place some seventy years after emancipation, and memories change over time.

Even with these limits, the collection remains one of the largest sources of firsthand testimony from people who lived through slavery. It preserves voices that were rarely included in official records, and it has shaped how scholars and the public understand that history. Today the narratives are held by the Library of Congress, and many can be read online.$q$, $q$The main purpose of the passage is to:$q$, $q$["describe a collection of interviews with formerly enslaved people, including its value and its limits", "explain how the Great Depression began", "argue that the Federal Writers' Project wasted money", "list the states where slavery was legal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage explains what the narratives are, why they matter, and how historians use them carefully.$q$),
