Even with these limits, the collection remains one of the largest sources of firsthand testimony from people who lived through slavery. It preserves voices that were rarely included in official records, and it has shaped how scholars and the public understand that history. Today the narratives are held by the Library of Congress, and many can be read online.$q$, $q$According to the passage, the interviews were recorded between:$q$, $q$["1929 and 1933", "1861 and 1865", "1936 and 1938", "1950 and 1955"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Paragraph 2 gives the dates.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Reasoning$q$, 'mc', $q$In the 1930s, during the Great Depression, the U.S. government hired thousands of out-of-work writers through a program called the Federal Writers' Project. Among their assignments was one that historians now consider priceless: interviewing elderly Americans who had been born into slavery.

