  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Author's view$q$, 'mc', $q$In the 1930s, during the Great Depression, the U.S. government hired thousands of out-of-work writers through a program called the Federal Writers' Project. Among their assignments was one that historians now consider priceless: interviewing elderly Americans who had been born into slavery.

Between 1936 and 1938, interviewers recorded the memories of more than two thousand formerly enslaved people across seventeen states. The interviewees described daily work, family life, punishment, escape, and the moment they learned they were free. Many were children when the Civil War ended, so their accounts reflect a child's view of slavery and its end.

Historians use these narratives carefully. Most interviewers were white, and in the segregated South, some interviewees may not have spoken freely to them. Interviewers sometimes wrote down speech in exaggerated dialect, and some edited the stories they collected. The interviews also took place some seventy years after emancipation, and memories change over time.

Even with these limits, the collection remains one of the largest sources of firsthand testimony from people who lived through slavery. It preserves voices that were rarely included in official records, and it has shaped how scholars and the public understand that history. Today the narratives are held by the Library of Congress, and many can be read online.$q$, $q$The author's overall view of the narratives is that they are:$q$, $q$["less important than official records", "too flawed to be useful", "completely accurate", "valuable despite their limitations"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Even with these limits, the collection remains one of the largest sources..."$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$Some of the world's most important crops depend on an animal most people never think about: the bat. In tropical and desert regions, many plants are pollinated by bats that feed on nectar. The agave plant, used to make a number of products in Mexico, is pollinated largely by nectar-feeding bats, and so are wild varieties of bananas and the durian fruit of Southeast Asia.

Bats also protect crops in another way: by eating insects. A single small bat can eat a large share of its body weight in insects in one night. In farming regions of the United States, insect-eating bats feed on moths whose caterpillars damage corn and cotton. Researchers who have tried to estimate the value of this "pest control" have concluded that bats save farmers billions of dollars a year by reducing crop damage and the need for pesticides.

Despite their usefulness, many bat populations are in trouble. In North America, a fungal disease called white-nose syndrome has killed millions of hibernating bats since it was first found in New York in 2006. Habitat loss and wind turbines, which can kill bats that fly near the spinning blades, add to the pressure.

Scientists are testing ways to help: treatments for the fungus, protecting caves where bats hibernate, and adjusting when wind turbines spin on nights when bats are most active. Their work suggests that protecting bats is not only good for wildlife but also for the farms that feed people.$q$, $q$The passage mainly argues that bats:$q$, $q$["are found only in tropical areas", "should be removed from farming regions", "are valuable to agriculture and face serious threats", "are dangerous to farm animals"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$It describes bats' value (pollination, pest control), then their threats and efforts to help.$q$),
