  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Structure$q$, 'mc', $q$Some of the world's most important crops depend on an animal most people never think about: the bat. In tropical and desert regions, many plants are pollinated by bats that feed on nectar. The agave plant, used to make a number of products in Mexico, is pollinated largely by nectar-feeding bats, and so are wild varieties of bananas and the durian fruit of Southeast Asia.

Bats also protect crops in another way: by eating insects. A single small bat can eat a large share of its body weight in insects in one night. In farming regions of the United States, insect-eating bats feed on moths whose caterpillars damage corn and cotton. Researchers who have tried to estimate the value of this "pest control" have concluded that bats save farmers billions of dollars a year by reducing crop damage and the need for pesticides.

Despite their usefulness, many bat populations are in trouble. In North America, a fungal disease called white-nose syndrome has killed millions of hibernating bats since it was first found in New York in 2006. Habitat loss and wind turbines, which can kill bats that fly near the spinning blades, add to the pressure.

Scientists are testing ways to help: treatments for the fungus, protecting caves where bats hibernate, and adjusting when wind turbines spin on nights when bats are most active. Their work suggests that protecting bats is not only good for wildlife but also for the farms that feed people.$q$, $q$The last paragraph mainly serves to:$q$, $q$["explain how bats find insects", "argue that wind turbines should be banned", "introduce a new threat to bats", "describe efforts to protect bats and connect them to the passage's main point"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$It lists solutions and ties bat protection to farming.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$The architect Julia Morgan designed more than seven hundred buildings in a career that lasted nearly fifty years, yet for much of the twentieth century her name was barely known outside California. Born in San Francisco in 1872, she studied civil engineering at the University of California, Berkeley, and then became the first woman admitted to the architecture program at the École des Beaux-Arts in Paris, one of the most respected design schools in the world.

Back in California, Morgan built a practice that ranged from small homes to churches, schools and community buildings. After the 1906 San Francisco earthquake, her use of reinforced concrete, which had helped one of her buildings survive the disaster, brought her more work. Her most famous project was Hearst Castle, a vast estate on the California coast that she designed and oversaw for nearly thirty years for the newspaper publisher William Randolph Hearst.

Morgan avoided publicity. She rarely gave interviews and is often quoted as saying that her buildings would speak for her. Some historians believe this choice, along with the fact that she was a woman in a field dominated by men, explains why she was long overlooked.

That has changed. In 2014, the American Institute of Architects awarded Morgan its highest honor, the AIA Gold Medal, making her the first woman to receive it. She had died in 1957.$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["the 1906 San Francisco earthquake", "an accomplished architect who was long overlooked and later honored", "how Hearst Castle was paid for", "the history of the École des Beaux-Arts"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$It covers Morgan's career, why she was overlooked, and her later recognition.$q$),
