Morgan avoided publicity. She rarely gave interviews and is often quoted as saying that her buildings would speak for her. Some historians believe this choice, along with the fact that she was a woman in a field dominated by men, explains why she was long overlooked.

That has changed. In 2014, the American Institute of Architects awarded Morgan its highest honor, the AIA Gold Medal, making her the first woman to receive it. She had died in 1957.$q$, $q$According to the passage, one reason Morgan received more work after the 1906 earthquake was that:$q$, $q$["she moved her office to Paris", "one of her reinforced-concrete buildings had survived it", "she had predicted the earthquake", "she lowered her prices"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Paragraph 2: reinforced concrete helped one of her buildings survive.$q$),
