Morgan avoided publicity. She rarely gave interviews and is often quoted as saying that her buildings would speak for her. Some historians believe this choice, along with the fact that she was a woman in a field dominated by men, explains why she was long overlooked.

That has changed. In 2014, the American Institute of Architects awarded Morgan its highest honor, the AIA Gold Medal, making her the first woman to receive it. She had died in 1957.$q$, $q$The last paragraph points out that Morgan's AIA Gold Medal was awarded:$q$, $q$["after her death", "before she designed Hearst Castle", "for her work in Paris", "when she was a student"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$She received it in 2014; she had died in 1957.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Details$q$, 'mc', $q$When ice forms on a pond in winter, it forms on top, and that simple fact makes life in the pond possible. Most substances become denser when they freeze; solid wax sinks in melted wax. Water is unusual. As water cools, it becomes denser until it reaches about 4°C. Below that temperature, it begins to expand, and when it freezes, ice is about nine percent less dense than liquid water. So ice floats.

This property shapes what happens in a lake as winter arrives. Surface water cools and, being denser, sinks, while warmer water rises to take its place. This mixing continues until the entire lake reaches about 4°C. After that, further cooling makes surface water lighter, not heavier, so it stays on top, gets colder, and eventually freezes. The ice then acts as a lid, insulating the water below, which often stays near 4°C all winter.

If ice sank, lakes would freeze from the bottom up. In cold climates, many would freeze solid, and fish and other organisms would have nowhere to survive. Instead, beneath a layer of ice, life continues slowly through the winter.

The reason for water's odd behavior lies in its molecules. As water freezes, hydrogen bonds lock the molecules into an open, six-sided pattern that takes up more space than the jumbled arrangement of the liquid.$q$, $q$According to the passage, liquid water is densest at about:$q$, $q$["0°C", "4°C", "−4°C", "9°C"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"It becomes denser until it reaches about 4°C."$q$),
