  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$Marcus had run the 800 meters in every meet that season, and in every meet he had finished third. Third was respectable. Third got you a ribbon and a nod from Coach Lin. But third, Marcus had decided, was also a habit, and he wanted to break it.

The problem, according to Coach, was the second lap. "You run the first lap like you're scared of it," she said, "and the second like you're angry at it." Marcus didn't see it that way. He thought he was being smart, saving energy. Coach handed him a stopwatch and told him to time the leaders' first laps at the next meet instead of running.

He sat in the bleachers, annoyed, clicking the watch. The winner ran the first lap only a second faster than Marcus usually did. But she didn't slow down in the second. She didn't speed up, either. She just kept going, as steady as a metronome, while the others faded and then sprinted wildly at the end.

At practice that week, Marcus ran laps with the watch in his hand, trying to make the two halves match. It felt wrong at first, too fast early and too easy late. At the last meet of the season he ran the plan anyway. He finished second, by a hand's width, behind a runner who had beaten him by three seconds a month before. Coach Lin said nothing, which from her was a compliment. He kept the stopwatch.

(Passage written for ScholarTasks practice.)$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["a runner who improves by learning to pace his race evenly", "a runner who decides to quit track", "a coach who is unfair to her runners", "the rules of the 800-meter race"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Marcus learns steady pacing from watching the winner and improves.$q$),
