  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$For most of history, people told time by the Sun. Each town set its clocks so that noon came when the Sun was highest in the sky, which meant that clocks in towns a few dozen miles apart could differ by several minutes. For travelers on foot or horseback, this hardly mattered.

Railroads changed that. By the mid-1800s, trains in the United States connected hundreds of towns, each with its own local time. Railroad companies kept their own schedules based on the time in their headquarters cities, so a single station might display several clocks showing different times for different lines. Missed connections, and even collisions, were blamed on the confusion.

On November 18, 1883, the major U.S. and Canadian railroads adopted a system of standard time zones. The continent was divided into broad bands, and every clock within a band was set to the same time. In many cities, local clocks were reset at noon that day, and some people called it "the day of two noons."

The railroads' system was not federal law at first. Many towns adopted it quickly, but others resisted, preferring to keep their own sun time. It was not until 1918 that Congress passed the Standard Time Act, officially establishing time zones in the United States. A change started by private companies for their own convenience had become the way an entire nation organized its days.$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["how sundials work", "how railroads led to the adoption of standard time zones", "a famous train collision in 1883", "why some towns refused to build railroads"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$It traces the shift from local sun time to railroad time zones to federal law.$q$),
