  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$The night before the science fair, Priya's volcano collapsed. It had taken her three weekends to build: chicken wire, newspaper strips soaked in flour paste, four coats of brown and gray paint. Now one whole side had slumped into a soggy heap on the kitchen table, and the baking-soda chamber inside was showing like a broken bone.

Her older brother, Dev, looked at it from the doorway. "You could just tell them it erupted," he said. Priya did not laugh.

What she wanted to do was give up. What she did instead, after crying for exactly ten minutes in the bathroom, was ask herself what had gone wrong. The paste, she decided. She had mixed it too wet on the last coat, and the layers underneath had never dried. She remembered her teacher, Mr. Ochoa, saying that a failed experiment was still an experiment, as long as you learned why it failed.

So at midnight she cut a new poster board. At the top she wrote, in careful letters: Why My Volcano Collapsed. She drew a diagram of the layers, labeled the wet one, and wrote three ways to fix it next time.

The next day, judges walked past a dozen perfect volcanoes to stop at the wreck on Priya's table. One of them read the poster twice. Priya didn't win first place. But on the ribbon she brought home, the one she kept, someone had written: Best Scientific Thinking.

(Passage written for ScholarTasks practice.)$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["a science teacher who judges a fair", "a brother and sister who build a volcano together", "a student who turns a failed project into a lesson and is recognized for her thinking", "how to build a model volcano"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Priya studies why the volcano failed, presents that, and wins a ribbon for scientific thinking.$q$),
