(Passage written for ScholarTasks practice.)$q$, $q$Which statement best expresses a theme of the passage?$q$, $q$["Learning from failure can be as valuable as success.", "Older siblings always give good advice.", "Winning first place is all that matters.", "Science projects should be finished early."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Mr. Ochoa's idea and the ribbon both show that learning from failure matters.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$In many countries, people vote on paper ballots that are counted by hand or scanned by machines. In others, voters touch screens. Across these systems, election officials share a central goal: results that are not only accurate but that the public trusts are accurate.

One tool used to build that trust is the post-election audit. After votes are counted, officials draw a random sample of paper ballots and check them by hand against the machine count. If the hand count matches closely, the audit supports the reported result. If it doesn't, officials examine more ballots.

A "risk-limiting audit" adjusts how many ballots are checked based on how close the race was. In a landslide, a relatively small random sample can show with high confidence that the winner really won. In a close race, many more ballots must be checked, and in a very close race the audit may expand to a full hand recount. The method is designed to limit the risk that a wrong outcome is certified.

Such audits require one thing above all: a paper record of each vote. A purely electronic system leaves nothing independent to check the machines against. For this reason, many election experts recommend that every voting system produce a paper ballot that the voter can see before it is cast.$q$, $q$The main purpose of the passage is to:$q$, $q$["describe the history of voting in one country", "argue that touch screens should replace paper ballots", "explain how post-election audits help confirm election results", "compare the costs of different voting machines"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The passage explains audits, risk-limiting audits, and why they need paper.$q$),
