  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Vocabulary in context$q$, 'mc', $q$In many countries, people vote on paper ballots that are counted by hand or scanned by machines. In others, voters touch screens. Across these systems, election officials share a central goal: results that are not only accurate but that the public trusts are accurate.

One tool used to build that trust is the post-election audit. After votes are counted, officials draw a random sample of paper ballots and check them by hand against the machine count. If the hand count matches closely, the audit supports the reported result. If it doesn't, officials examine more ballots.

A "risk-limiting audit" adjusts how many ballots are checked based on how close the race was. In a landslide, a relatively small random sample can show with high confidence that the winner really won. In a close race, many more ballots must be checked, and in a very close race the audit may expand to a full hand recount. The method is designed to limit the risk that a wrong outcome is certified.

Such audits require one thing above all: a paper record of each vote. A purely electronic system leaves nothing independent to check the machines against. For this reason, many election experts recommend that every voting system produce a paper ballot that the voter can see before it is cast.$q$, $q$As used in the passage, "landslide" most nearly means:$q$, $q$["a tie", "a recount", "a win by a very large margin", "a sudden fall of earth and rock"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A landslide is contrasted with a close race, so it means a big win.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Details$q$, 'mc', $q$Fireflies are not flies; they are beetles. Their glow comes from a chemical reaction inside special cells in the abdomen. A molecule called luciferin combines with oxygen with the help of an enzyme called luciferase, and the reaction releases energy as light. Very little of the energy is given off as heat, which is why scientists sometimes call firefly light "cold light."

For fireflies, light is mostly a language. In many North American species, males fly through the evening air flashing a pattern specific to their species, and females on the ground answer with their own timed flash. A male that sees the right answer flies toward her and the conversation continues. Because each species has its own pattern of flash length, number and timing, the signals help fireflies find mates of their own kind.

Some predators take advantage of this. Females of certain firefly species can mimic the answering flashes of other species. When a male of the other species flies down expecting a mate, the mimic eats him.

Firefly numbers appear to be declining in many places. Scientists point to habitat loss, pesticides and light pollution. Artificial lights at night can drown out the flashes fireflies use to find each other, much as loud music can drown out a conversation.$q$, $q$According to the passage, firefly light is called "cold light" because:$q$, $q$["the reaction gives off very little heat", "the reaction takes place outside the body", "fireflies glow only on cold nights", "the light is blue"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Paragraph 1 explains this.$q$),
