  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$In 1955, nine months before Rosa Parks's famous arrest, a fifteen-year-old student named Claudette Colvin refused to give up her seat on a segregated bus in Montgomery, Alabama. Colvin had been studying Black history at school, and when the driver ordered her to move, she later said, she felt that history had her pinned to the seat. She was arrested and taken to jail.

Civil rights leaders in Montgomery had been looking for a case to challenge bus segregation, and for a time they considered building a protest around Colvin's. They ultimately decided against it. Leaders worried that a teenager might not hold up under the pressure of a public campaign, and when Colvin became pregnant later that year, some feared that opponents would use her personal life to discredit the cause. When Rosa Parks, a respected adult activist and secretary of the local NAACP chapter, was arrested that December, leaders chose her case to launch the Montgomery bus boycott.

Colvin's role, however, did not end there. In 1956 she was one of four plaintiffs in Browder v. Gayle, the federal lawsuit in which a court ruled that bus segregation in Montgomery was unconstitutional. The U.S. Supreme Court upheld that ruling later the same year, and the boycott ended soon after.

For decades, Colvin's story was little known. In recent years, books and news coverage have brought it wider attention, and in 2021, a court in Alabama granted her request to have her juvenile record cleared.$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["the life of Rosa Parks", "the history of public transportation in Alabama", "how the NAACP was founded", "Claudette Colvin's early act of protest and her important but long-overlooked role in ending bus segregation"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The passage follows Colvin's arrest, why leaders chose another case, her role in the lawsuit, and later recognition.$q$),
