  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$Most people imagine the deep ocean as a cold, dark desert. In 1977, scientists exploring the sea floor near the Galápagos Islands in the submersible Alvin found something no one expected: oases of life clustered around cracks in the sea floor where hot, mineral-rich water poured out.

These hydrothermal vents form where seawater seeps into the crust, is heated by magma below, and rises back up loaded with dissolved minerals. Some vents release water hotter than 350°C; it does not boil because of the enormous pressure at those depths. As the hot water meets near-freezing seawater, minerals settle out and build tall chimneys.

Around the vents, scientists found giant tube worms, clams and crabs living in great numbers. That raised a puzzle. Nearly all life on Earth's surface depends, directly or indirectly, on energy from sunlight, captured by plants and algae through photosynthesis. No sunlight reaches the deep sea floor. The answer turned out to be bacteria that use chemical energy from compounds such as hydrogen sulfide in the vent water to make food, a process called chemosynthesis. Giant tube worms have no mouth or stomach; instead, bacteria living inside their bodies make food for them.

The discovery changed ideas about where life can exist. If life can thrive without sunlight on Earth's sea floor, some scientists reason, it might also exist in dark oceans beneath the icy surfaces of moons such as Jupiter's Europa.$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["the history of the Galápagos Islands", "how submarines are built", "the discovery of life around deep-sea vents and how it survives without sunlight", "why the ocean is salty"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$It covers the discovery, how vents work, chemosynthesis, and what it means.$q$),
