  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$The quilts made by the women of Gee's Bend, Alabama, a small, mostly Black community on a bend of the Alabama River, were for generations simply part of daily life. Women pieced them from whatever cloth they had: worn work clothes, feed sacks, scraps of dresses. Many of the quilters learned from mothers and grandmothers, and the craft passed down through families for more than a century.

What set the quilts apart was their design. Instead of following traditional patterns exactly, many Gee's Bend quilters improvised bold arrangements of color and shape: off-center blocks, strips of different widths, patches of denim faded to many shades of blue. A pattern might begin in a familiar form and then shift as the quilter worked with the fabric at hand.

In the early 2000s, an exhibition of Gee's Bend quilts traveled to major museums in the United States, including the Whitney Museum of American Art in New York. Critics compared the quilts to works by famous modern painters, and the exhibition drew large crowds. The quilters, some of whom had never traveled far from home, were invited to speak at openings.

The attention brought new respect, and some income, to the community. It also raised questions that the quilters themselves have discussed openly: who decides what counts as art, and who should benefit when everyday objects become museum pieces?$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["how to make a quilt", "a community's quilting tradition and its recognition as art", "the geography of the Alabama River", "the history of the Whitney Museum"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$It describes the tradition, the design, museum recognition, and questions it raised.$q$),
