  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$Before refrigerators, before canning, people still needed to keep food through the winter. One of the oldest solutions is fermentation, in which microorganisms such as bacteria and yeast break down sugars in food. The process can preserve food for months, and it produces flavors that many cultures prize.

In sauerkraut, Korean kimchi and many kinds of pickles, bacteria called lactobacilli convert sugars into lactic acid. As the acid builds up, it makes the food sour and creates conditions in which most harmful bacteria cannot grow. Salt plays a key role: it draws water out of the vegetables and slows the growth of unwanted microbes while lactobacilli, which tolerate salt well, take over.

Yeast drives a different kind of fermentation. In bread dough, yeast consumes sugars and releases carbon dioxide gas, which makes the dough rise. The same basic process, with different ingredients and conditions, produces alcohol in other foods and drinks.

Fermentation is not only about preservation. Many fermented foods are easier to digest than their raw ingredients, and scientists are studying whether the live bacteria in some fermented foods benefit the microbes that live in the human gut. Early research is promising, though researchers caution that many claims made in advertising go beyond what has been shown.$q$, $q$The passage is mainly about:$q$, $q$["how to grow cabbage", "how fermentation preserves and changes food", "the invention of the refrigerator", "why salt is unhealthy"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$It explains fermentation, its types, and its benefits.$q$),
