  ('ACT', $q$ACT Reading$q$, $q$Main idea$q$, 'mc', $q$Many people assume that the best way to remember something is to review it again and again until it feels familiar. Research on learning suggests that feeling familiar can be misleading. Rereading notes makes material seem easier, but the ease often reflects recognition, not the ability to recall information without help.

A more effective strategy, according to many studies, is retrieval practice: trying to pull information from memory, for example by answering practice questions or explaining a topic without looking at notes. In a typical experiment, one group of students reads a passage several times while another group reads it once and then writes down everything they can remember. On a test a week later, the second group usually remembers more, even though they spent less time with the passage and often predicted they would do worse.

A second strategy, spacing, means spreading study sessions out over days or weeks instead of cramming. Forgetting a little between sessions seems to make the next round of recall more effortful, and that effort strengthens memory.

These strategies can feel harder than rereading, which may be why students rarely choose them on their own. Researchers sometimes call this a "desirable difficulty": a challenge that slows learning down in the moment but makes it last.$q$, $q$The main purpose of the passage is to:$q$, $q$["argue that students should study less", "describe the history of education research", "explain how to write a good essay", "explain why effortful study strategies like retrieval practice and spacing work better than rereading"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The passage compares rereading with retrieval practice and spacing.$q$),
