A second strategy, spacing, means spreading study sessions out over days or weeks instead of cramming. Forgetting a little between sessions seems to make the next round of recall more effortful, and that effort strengthens memory.

These strategies can feel harder than rereading, which may be why students rarely choose them on their own. Researchers sometimes call this a "desirable difficulty": a challenge that slows learning down in the moment but makes it last.$q$, $q$According to the passage, in the typical experiment, students who practiced recalling the passage:$q$, $q$["remembered less than the rereading group", "spent more time with the passage", "remembered more a week later even though they expected to do worse", "forgot everything within a day"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Paragraph 2 describes these results.$q$),
